{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>You may make it a freewill offering</b> for the Temple treasury, <b>but for a vow it shall not be accepted</b> for the altar. Which is the consecrated animal that comes for acceptance? You must say: this is a consecration for the altar. This is the language of Rashi. According to this, we must interpret \"you may make it a freewill offering, but for a vow it shall not be accepted\" as follows: he says that one may make it either a vow or a freewill offering for the Temple treasury, but it will not find favor on the altar, for there is no difference at all between a vow and a freewill offering in this matter — both are permitted for the Temple treasury and forbidden for the altar. Scripture already mentioned this regarding the burnt offering: \"for all their vows and for all their freewill offerings\" (Leviticus 22:18), and regarding the peace offerings: \"either to fulfill a vow or as a freewill offering\" (Leviticus 22:21), and so is the midrash of our Sages in Torat Kohanim (chapter 7:6).\n\nIt is possible to settle the language of Scripture thus: because most freewill offerings are for the Temple treasury, as He said regarding the Tabernacle: \"everyone whose heart inspired him brought the offering of HaShem\" (Exodus 35:5), and so regarding the Temple: \"in the uprightness of my heart I have willingly offered\" (I Chronicles 29:17), and so in the Second Temple: \"along with the freewill offering for the house of Elohim\" (Ezra 1:4), and so it is written regarding the Temple treasury: \"all the silver that any man's heart prompts him to bring to the house of HaShem\" (II Kings 12:5) — and that is the freewill offering. The reason is that in the matter of the Temple treasury there is no obligation, only the freewill offering of the soul. And the practice of those who give to the Temple treasury is that they bring it by way of freewill offering — \"behold, this is for the building.\" Therefore, every place where Scripture mentions \"freewill offering\" without specification, it is for the Temple treasury, until it specifies in it \"for a burnt offering\" or \"for a sacrifice of peace offerings,\" as it mentioned above (verses 18, 21). And \"a vow\" said without specification refers to consecrated offerings of the altar: \"I will offer a sacrifice of thanksgiving\" (Psalms 116:17), \"I will pay my vows to HaShem, indeed now, in the presence of all His people, in the courts of the house of HaShem\" (ibid. 18–19), \"I will come to Your house with burnt offerings, I will pay my vows to You\" (ibid. 66:13), \"Upon me, O Elohim, are Your vows; I will pay them, thanksgivings to You\" (ibid. 56:13). And those who bring to the altar by way of vows say: if Elohim grants me success, I will come before Him with a burnt offering. Therefore Scripture says here: an ox or a sheep that is torn or severed — you may make it a freewill offering, for whatever your heart prompts, but for a vow to HaShem it shall not be accepted, as He said (Leviticus 22:20): \"You shall not offer these, for they will not be accepted favorably for you.\" For Scripture did not say \"you shall offer it as a freewill offering,\" in the language used throughout the entire passage, but rather said \"you shall make it\" — meaning, you shall make of it whatever you find your hand able to do with your strength, but for a vow, for favor it shall not be.\n\nThe Sages were compelled to say thus, for it is impossible in any way that all the blemishes — the wart and the eczema and even the crushed and the bruised — should be forbidden for every offering, whether a vow or a freewill offering, while the torn and the severed are forbidden in some cases and permitted in others, and such a thing is not found in the Torah among the blemishes of those who bring offerings or regarding impurity, to distinguish among the offerings. Therefore they said that \"you shall make it\" is not like \"you shall offer it.\"\n\nIt is also possible that He said: an ox or a sheep that is torn or severed is permitted to be made a freewill offering — to say \"behold, this one\" — but if he vowed \"an ox or a sheep is upon me\" — if he brings a blemished one it will not be accepted for him and he is not exempt from his vow. And let us say according to the way of our Sages that both of them are for the Temple treasury, for \"you shall make it\" is not like \"you shall offer it\" — and this is true without doubt.\n\nScripture mentioned the permissibility of a freewill offering for the Temple treasury in the case of the torn and the severed, which are blemishes of formation from the womb, and all the more so the minor blemishes of acquired nature — blindness in which the eye has become dimmed, and the broken — and there is no need to mention the wart and the eczema.\n\nIt is possible that Scripture connects: \"blind or broken or maimed or with a wart or eczema or a wen, and any ox or sheep that is torn or severed — you may make a freewill offering\" for each one of these, \"but for a vow none of them shall be accepted,\" even if they went up, they came down.\n\nIn Torat Kohanim (chapter 7:5): From where to include all the disqualifications in an ox or a sheep? Scripture says: \"and any ox or sheep that is torn or severed\" — to include all the disqualifications in an ox or a sheep. And our Sages (Temurah 7a) further derived from the generalization of \"you may make it a freewill offering\" — that it may be made a freewill offering for whatever one wishes, but not the unblemished ones, for one who consecrates them to the Temple treasury transgresses a positive commandment."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:32.381129892Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}